Native 120 FPS: cheaper frames, a fairer host check, and a profiler that
says where the time goes.

Reported: 120 FPS "can't keep 120 and drops to 60" at 2x internal resolution
on Direct3D 12. The session's heartbeat: 5 engages, 4 fallbacks, all four
host fallbacks - the machine could not produce 120 fields a second - with
level-code compilation working again (0043). Frames at 120 are bound by the
emulation thread: the game does ~1M PS1 cycles of work per field (0.9M at 60,
where 400% clock changes nothing in stock-equivalent terms), and emulation,
GPU emulation, recording and present share one 8.3 ms slot.

Renderer (both APIs):
  - PSX_GL_SEMI_BATCH on by default. Consecutive dual-source semi prims
    (batch key 4: no mask check, not mode 2) share one draw. Checked
    byte-identical to isolated draws in tuning/renderer_parity - overlapping
    prims of one mode, modes 0/1/3 interleaved with an opaque prim between -
    on GL and Direct3D 12, 1x-3x, nearest and bilinear, with and without
    post-processing. Halves the draws of a semi-heavy frame; recording -20%.
    PSX_GL_SEMI_BATCH=0 isolates again.

Direct3D 12 (gpu_gl12.cpp): the command list remembers what it has bound -
pipeline state, render target, viewport/scissor, the SRV table (reused while
the same four views are bound, keyed by an SRV-slot generation so a rewritten
slot misses), the sampler table, both constant buffers (per-program content
versions from one global counter, moved only when a uniform write changes
bytes), topology, blend factor, stencil ref - and a draw sets only what
changed. Reset per command list and after the flip. Resource transitions are
still checked on every draw. Recording on the parity bench: 0.59-0.65 ms ->
0.39-0.42 ms a frame together with the batching (GL 0.31 -> 0.26); VRAM
bit-identical to GL and the debug layer silent in 12 configurations.

crash2_60fps.h: the host check at 120 has two tiers. A window under
C2_120_HOST_FAR_HZ (108, 90%) steps down at once - that is slow motion anyone
sees; one just short of 114 gets a second window, since a single hitch (a
shader, a level-code DLL being published) can cost one. The rate of the
window that stepped down is kept (native_120fps_fail_hz), so the Play page can
say how many of the 120 the machine reached.

host_profiler.c (new): a sampling profiler for the emulation thread. A helper
thread suspends it ~500 times a second, reads the instruction pointer and
resumes it - no locks while it is suspended - tagged with what the thread was
doing (emulating, presenting, pacing; main.cpp marks them). 120 FPS profiles
its first 20 s engaged and writes psx_host_profile.json next to the
heartbeat; PSX_HOST_PROFILE=1 profiles a whole session, =0 never.
tuning/scripts/host_profile.py names the addresses from the executable's own
symbol table and splits the time by function and phase. The next 120 session
therefore reports exactly what stands between this machine and 120.

No file here is in codegen_hash_sources.cmake; overlay shards are unaffected.

